<Base title="Privacy policy" description="What Kitchen Watcher collects (very little), why, who processes it, and how to ask us to delete anything we hold about you." crumbs={[{ name: 'Privacy', path: '/privacy/' }]}>
  <article>
    <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="dek">Short version: no ad trackers, and one analytics tool (Google Analytics) so we can see which pages are read. Here is exactly what happens.</p>
    <p class="meta">Last updated <time datetime="2026-09-28">September 28, 2026</time></p>

    <h2>When you read the site</h2>
    <p>Kitchen Watcher is hosted on Cloudflare. Like any web host, Cloudflare processes the technical details of each request, such as your IP address, browser type and the page requested, to deliver the page and protect the site from attacks. We don't use that data to identify you, and we don't run advertising scripts. Cloudflare's own handling is described in the <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare privacy policy</a>.</p>
    <p>Site search runs entirely in your browser; what you type is not sent to us.</p>

    <h2>Analytics</h2>
    <p>We use Google Analytics 4 to count visits and see which pages are read, how readers arrive (for example from a search engine) and roughly where they are (country or city level). Google Analytics sets first-party cookies (named <code>_ga</code>) to tell a returning browser from a new one. We don't send Google your name or email, we haven't turned on advertising features or Google Signals, and we don't use this data to show you ads. Google's handling is described in <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites">how Google uses information from sites that use its services</a>. Google Analytics and any other measurement tags are loaded through Google Tag Manager, which itself doesn't collect personal data; if we add another tool through it, we'll name it on this page first. You can block these with any mainstream content blocker or with Google's <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout">Analytics opt-out browser add-on</a>.</p>

    <h2>When you click a shop link</h2>
    <p>On pages with retailer links, a small script records which product link was clicked and on which page, so we know which pages people find useful. It doesn't record who you are. The retailer you land on has its own privacy policy and may use cookies to credit us for a purchase.</p>

    <h2>Email</h2>
    <p>We plan to offer price-drop alerts and a newsletter. They will only ever be sent to people who sign up for them, each email will have an unsubscribe link, and we will list the email providers we use on this page before either launches.</p>

    <h2>Your requests</h2>
    <p>You can ask what we hold about you, or ask us to delete it, through our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>. We don't hold names or emails from simply reading the site; the analytics data sits with Google and is not linked to your identity.</p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>Kitchen Watcher is written for adults and doesn't knowingly collect information from children.</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>If this policy changes, we'll update the date at the top of this page.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
